<SddRoot keywords="root, portal, vision, scope-graph, idempotent, project-setup, infra-bootstrap" type="directive" ver="2.0">
  <Mission>
    Owner of `specs/README.md` — the Project Portal: Vision + Scope Graph + Scopes table.
    Idempotent: runs any number of times to refine vision, extend the graph, or bootstrap the
    first infra-base spec. Loaded lazily by the router; the router-core beliefs it relies on are
    declared in `BeliefState deps` (inherited, not repeated).

    Input — operator intent at project level. Output:
    - `specs/README.md` with Vision + Scope Graph + Scopes table.
    - Optional minimal `specs/infra-base/infra-base.spec.md` when the operator declared tooling.

    When the portal is absent, root first probes the repo (`sdd-state --probe`): a greenfield repo (no
    code) is initialized here; a repo that already holds code is handed to the from-code recovery branch
    (`discover-from-code`) — root never invents a vision over code that already exists.

    Out of scope: design of any individual scope (its vision, architecture, requirements,
    modules) — that is the scope branch. Root writes only the project-level map.
  </Mission>

  <BeliefState deps="AX_OPERATOR_LANGUAGE, AX_TOOL_INVOCATION, AX_OPERATOR_DIALOGUE_STYLE, AX_DIALOGUE_DISCIPLINE, AX_OPERATOR_SAFEGUARD, AX_NO_PROCESS_NARRATION, AX_STATELESS_ARTIFACT">
    {{> "axiom/spec/ax-portal-primary-owner"}}

    {{> "axiom/scaffold/ax-idempotent-modes"}}

    {{> "axiom/spec/ax-scope-graph-discipline"}}

    {{> "axiom/infra/ax-infra-base-bootstrap"}}

    {{> "axiom/spec/ax-scope-table-format"}}

    {{> "axiom/spec/ax-vision-only-for-application"}}

    {{> "axiom/process/ax-close-with-integrity-check"}}
  </BeliefState>

  <HaltConditions>
    | 🛑 ID | Trigger |
    |---|---|
    | `H_NO_INTENT` | Operator invoked the root flow with empty intake against an existing Portal |
    | `H_CYCLE_IN_GRAPH` | Scope Graph contains a cycle (detected by `sdd-check`) |
    | `H_SCOPE_VS_MODULE_AMBIGUITY` | Operator labels as «scope» something that resembles a module |
    | `H_OPERATOR_REJECT` | Operator rejected Vision, Scope Graph, or the final Portal |
  </HaltConditions>

  <ExecutionPlan>
    <Step id="STEP_0_INTAKE">
      <Goal>Determine today's change set from state.</Goal>
      <Action>
        State from the router (`sdd-state`) already reports whether `specs/README.md` exists.
        - Absent → no portal yet: run `sdd-state --probe` to learn whether the repo is greenfield or
          already holds code (`AX_TOOL_INVOCATION`; probe only now that it is needed — not at flow start).
          - `CODE=absent` (empty / only package.json) → **greenfield init**. Ask (Russian): «Как называется
            проект? Опиши в одном предложении его vision и первые scope'ы (infra-base, backend, web — на
            черновую).»
          - `CODE=present` → **from-code recovery** — do NOT invent a vision over existing code:
            `READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/discover-from-code.directive.xml")`.
        - Present → empty intake → `H_NO_INTENT`; otherwise classify the change:
          vision / add scope / update graph / infra bootstrap.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_1_VISION">
      <Goal>Lock the project-level Vision.</Goal>
      <Action>
        No product / library scope → skip Vision (`AX_VISION_ONLY_FOR_APPLICATION`). Else propose
        a one-sentence Vision: what we build and which problem it solves. Approval Check. STOP.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_2_SCOPE_GRAPH">
      <Goal>Place each scope by its relationships first, then its edges.</Goal>
      <Action>
        For each scope the relationship question comes FIRST (`AX_OPERATOR_SAFEGUARD`): who will depend
        on it, what it depends on, and whether it is cross-cutting (used by ≥2 scopes). Probe narrow
        framing — «авторизация только для web? а API / будущие клиенты?». The answer drives three things:
        the «scope vs module» test (a thing used inside one scope may be a module, not a scope →
        `H_SCOPE_VS_MODULE_AMBIGUITY`); the `depends-on` edges; and, when it is cross-cutting, a note that
        cross-scope scenarios or a contracts scope may be needed. If the change REMOVES or RENAMES a
        scope, first surface what it orphans — its `specs/<scope>/…` spec, tasks, and inbound references
        — and resolve with the operator (move / delete / repoint); never silently leave orphans. Then
        propose scope-type with rationale and render the Mermaid `graph TD`. Approval Check. STOP.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_3_INFRA_BOOTSTRAP">
      <Goal>Materialize the minimal infra-base spec if tooling was declared.</Goal>
      <Action>
        No tooling declared → skip. Else `READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/formats/infra-base-minimal-spec.xml")`
        and write `specs/infra-base/infra-base.spec.md` to that format (scope-type infrastructure,
        one-sentence Vision, Tool Stack table, note pointing to `discovery infra-base` for the full
        Decision Log). Approval Check. STOP.
      </Action>
    </Step>
    <Step id="STEP_4_PORTAL_WRITE">
      <Goal>Write / update `specs/README.md`.</Goal>
      <Action>
        `READ_AND_USE_DIRECTIVE("ai/directives/sdd-v2/formats/portal-structure.xml")` and assemble the
        Portal to that format. Scope status (✅ / 🚧) comes from `sdd-state` (presence of
        `specs/<scope>/<scope>.spec.md`), not a manual stat. Reconcile any placeholder rows left by the
        scope flow into full rows. Write the file. Then run `sdd-check --all .` to verify portal integrity —
        graph acyclicity, graph↔table↔spec coherence, no orphan specs or dangling scope references — and
        resolve any finding before finishing. Approval Check. STOP.
      </Action>
    </Step>
  </ExecutionPlan>

  <ChatOutput>
    Service line — dim, internal, never narrated (per `AX_NO_PROCESS_NARRATION`):
    `🗺️ ROOT_R<N> · <project> · <mode> · conf=<LOW|MEDIUM|HIGH>`. Breadcrumb milestones for this
    flow (per `BREADCRUMB_FORMAT`): Vision · Scope Graph · Infra · Portal.

